¥lines‘‚¤textÙnFor Moses of old time hath in every city them that preach him, being read in the synagogues every sabbath day.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÔThen pleased it the apostles and elders, with the whole church, to send chosen men of their own company to Antioch with Paul and Barnabas; namely, Judas surnamed Barsabas, and Silas, chief men among the brethren:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ²and they wrote letters by them after this manner; The apostles and elders and brethren send greeting unto the brethren which are of the Gentiles in Antioch and Syria and Cilicia:¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙËforasmuch as we have heard, that certain which went out from us have troubled you with words, subverting your souls, saying, Ye must be circumcised, and keep the law: to whom we gave no such commandment:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙxit seemed good unto us, being assembled with one accord, to send chosen men unto you with our beloved Barnabas and Paul,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙImen that have hazarded their lives for the name of our Lord Jesus Christ.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙYWe have sent therefore Judas and Silas, who shall also tell you the same things by mouth.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙoFor it seemed good to the Holy Ghost, and to us, to lay upon you no greater burden than these necessary things;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¯that ye abstain from meats offered to idols, and from blood, and from things strangled, and from fornication: from which if ye keep yourselves, ye shall do well. Fare ye well.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse#¦verses–„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙSo when they were dismissed, they came to Antioch: and when they had gathered the multitude together, they delivered the epistle:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ<which when they had read, they rejoiced for the consolation.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙoAnd Judas and Silas, being prophets also themselves, exhorted the brethren with many words, and confirmed them.¦layout ªlast_verse „¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙhAnd after they had tarried there a space, they were let go in peace from the brethren unto the apostles.¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ6Notwithstanding it pleased Silas to abide there still.¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙpPaul also and Barnabas continued in Antioch, teaching and preaching the word of the Lord, with many others also.¦layout ªlast_verse#…¤book+§chapter«first_verse$ªlast_verse&¦verses“„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ£And some days after Paul said unto Barnabas, Let us go again and visit our brethren in every city where we have preached the word of the Lord, and see how they do.¦layout ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙGAnd Barnabas determined to take with them John, whose surname was Mark.¦layout ªlast_verse%„¥verse&¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ{But Paul thought not good to take him with them, who departed from them from Pamphylia, and went not with them to the work.¦layout ªlast_verse&…¤book+§chapter«first_verse'ªlast_verse)¦verses“„¥verse'¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd the contention was so sharp between them, that they departed asunder one from the other: and so Barnabas took Mark, and sailed unto Cyprus;¦layout ªlast_verse'„¥verse(¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ\and Paul chose Silas, and departed, being recommended by the brethren unto the grace of God.¦layout ªlast_verse(„¥verse)¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ?And he went through Syria and Cilicia, confirming the churches.¦layout ªlast_verse)